WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.766
ShellCheck is most valuable when feedback
arrives early.

2
00:00:05.846 --> 00:00:12.616
If developers see findings while editing
or before opening a pull request,

3
00:00:12.696 --> 00:00:15.060
fixes are small and cheap.

4
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.620
If the first signal appears during a
production deployment,

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.494
the tool is being used too late.

6
00:00:23.574 --> 00:00:28.302
Local use, editor integration, pre-review
checks,

7
00:00:28.382 --> 00:00:35.475
and CI/CD linting all help move
script-quality feedback closer to the author.

8
00:00:35.555 --> 00:00:42.862
Teams should decide which scripts are in
scope and how strict the workflow should

9
00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:43.264
be.

10
00:00:43.344 --> 00:00:49.899
New production scripts may be expected to
pass ShellCheck before merge.

11
00:00:49.979 --> 00:00:55.890
Legacy scripts may need a baseline and
incremental cleanup plan.

12
00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:00.591
Some repositories may treat findings as
blocking;

13
00:01:00.671 --> 00:01:07.226
others may begin with reporting while the
team works through old issues.

14
00:01:07.306 --> 00:01:13.431
The right policy depends on the codebase,
but it should be explicit.

15
00:01:13.511 --> 00:01:20.388
Pull request review is a strong place for
ShellCheck because the tool and the

16
00:01:20.468 --> 00:01:23.477
reviewer can support each other.

17
00:01:23.557 --> 00:01:27.426
ShellCheck catches repeatable patterns.

18
00:01:27.506 --> 00:01:34.168
Humans evaluate intent, operational
impact, and safe failure behavior.

19
00:01:34.248 --> 00:01:38.547
A reviewer can ask why a warning was
suppressed,

20
00:01:38.627 --> 00:01:42.495
whether a quoted value changes semantics,

21
00:01:42.575 --> 00:01:46.874
whether a shell dialect is declared
correctly,

22
00:01:46.954 --> 00:01:52.649
and whether the script has been tested in
the intended runtime.

23
00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:57.027
CI/CD integration helps prevent
regressions.

24
00:01:57.107 --> 00:02:01.943
It makes shell-script quality visible,
repeatable,

25
00:02:02.023 --> 00:02:05.784
and less dependent on individual memory.

26
00:02:05.864 --> 00:02:12.527
But ShellCheck should be part of a broader
process that includes testing,

27
00:02:12.607 --> 00:02:16.045
review, and operational validation.

28
00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:21.391
Static analysis can raise the floor for
shell automation,

29
00:02:21.471 --> 00:02:28.778
but teams still need to understand what
the script does and how it behaves when the

30
00:02:28.858 --> 00:02:30.900
environment changes.
